\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



The vertices inherit the well-order of $\alpha$. An independent set of order type $\alpha$ must preserve that ordering, not just cardinality. An infinite path is a one-way infinite simple path; a finite path of each length is not by itself an infinite path.

A graph has a vertex set $V$ and edges that are unordered pairs of distinct vertices. A subgraph need not be induced unless that word is stated. A proper vertex colouring gives adjacent vertices different colours; $\chi(G)$ is the least number of colours. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/601}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For which limit ordinals $\alpha$ is it true that if $G$ is a graph with vertex set $\alpha$ then $G$ must have either an infinite path or independent set on a set of vertices with order type $\alpha$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Resolve the first open ordinal and characterize all limit ordinals. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Which limit ordinals ensure that every graph on that ordinal has either an infinite path or an independent set preserving the entire order type?

